  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Deep layer nerly flow cont this morning for ENC.\nHigh pres remains in control today with mainly dry weather\ncontinuing. May see a few iso showers on the sea/sound breezes\njust inland from the coast as some low/mid level moisture\nremains, but upper forcing is negligible and no impacts are\nexpected. Next appreciable chance of rain arrives Mon and Tue \nas yet another frontal boundary pushes southward. For Mon, the\nrain chances are confined to mainly interior zones as the front\nhas trended slower and later in the day. Tue appears the best\nchance for widespread showers and embedded thunderstorms as PW's\nsurge to aoa 2\" and frontal boundary is draped acrs ENC, and\nthus pops raised to 60-70%.\n\nKey Message 2...By mid week, strong high pressure system \nbecomes anchored acrs New England and ern Canada, while at the \nsame time broad low pres develops off the Mid Atlantic coast, \ntightening the pressure gradient along the mid- atlantic and \nnortheast. This setup will usher in stronger NE winds into\ncoastal ENC. Exact timing, placement, and strength of the low\nwill dictate wind speeds and impacts to coastal ENC. 20/00Z ECM\ncont to trend stronger with gradient winds and would suggest\nwidespread gales for the coast, though still a wide spread in\nensemble guidance from both ECM and AIFS, so details yet to be\nironed out. Despite the wind uncertainty, what is certain is\nthat a long fetch of tightened nerly gradient winds offshore\nwill bring high wave energy towards vulnerable portions of the \nOuter Banks. It is too soon to determine specifics at this point\nand trends will have to be monitored in the coming days.",
